            <td style="padding:10px 12px;color:#fff;font-weight:600;">{m.product}</td>
            <td style="padding:10px 12px;color:#fff;font-family:var(--mono);white-space:nowrap;">{m.entry}</td>
            <td style="padding:10px 12px;color:var(--text-3);">{m.strength}</td>
          </tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p class="text-gray-400" style="font-size:.85rem;margin-top:-0.5rem;">
    Starting rates as published by each vendor and gathered for this page. None has been read from the vendor's own
    pricing page under the standard set out in <a href="/how-we-test/" style="color:var(--g);text-decoration:underline">how we test</a>,
    so treat them as indicative bands rather than quotes — and note that several of these tools price on SKU count or
    order volume, so the entry figure is a floor, not a forecast of your bill.
  </p>

  <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3" style="margin-top:2rem;">Prediko and Inventory Planner, the two most-recommended</h3>
  <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
    These are the two names that come up most, they sit within a dollar of each other at entry, and the choice between
    them is genuinely close — so it is worth separating them properly rather than repeating both vendors' marketing.
  </p>
  <div style="overflow-x:auto;margin:1.5rem 0;">
    <table style="width:100%;border-collapse:collapse;font-size:.92rem;">
      <thead>
        <tr style="border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">
          <th style="text-align:left;padding:10px 12px;color:var(--text-3);font-weight:600;"></th>
          <th style="text-align:left;padding:10px 12px;color:#fff;font-weight:600;">Prediko</th>
          <th style="text-align:left;padding:10px 12px;color:#fff;font-weight:600;">Inventory Planner</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr style="border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">
          <td style="padding:10px 12px;color:var(--text-3);font-weight:600;">Entry</td>
          <td style="padding:10px 12px;color:#fff;font-family:var(--mono);">$119/mo</td>
          <td style="padding:10px 12px;color:#fff;font-family:var(--mono);">$119.99/mo (Shopify-only)</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">
          <td style="padding:10px 12px;color:var(--text-3);font-weight:600;">Scaling</td>
          <td style="padding:10px 12px;color:var(--text-3);">Plan-based, scales with requirements</td>
          <td style="padding:10px 12px;color:var(--text-3);">By SKU count — roughly $299 at ~1,000 SKUs, $599 at ~3,000</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">
          <td style="padding:10px 12px;color:var(--text-3);font-weight:600;">Owner</td>
          <td style="padding:10px 12px;color:var(--text-3);">Independent</td>
          <td style="padding:10px 12px;color:var(--text-3);">Sage</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">
          <td style="padding:10px 12px;color:var(--text-3);font-weight:600;">Character</td>
          <td style="padding:10px 12px;color:var(--text-3);">Newer, AI-forward, Shopify-native feel</td>
          <td style="padding:10px 12px;color:var(--text-3);">Established planning platform with a buying-team workflow</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">
          <td style="padding:10px 12px;color:var(--text-3);font-weight:600;">Distinctive strength</td>
          <td style="padding:10px 12px;color:var(--text-3);">Bundle inventory across locations; 70+ WMS and 3PL integrations</td>
          <td style="padding:10px 12px;color:var(--text-3);">Open-to-Buy budgeting; multi-channel depth</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">
          <td style="padding:10px 12px;color:var(--text-3);font-weight:600;">Onboarding</td>
          <td style="padding:10px 12px;color:var(--text-3);">14-day full-featured trial</td>
          <td style="padding:10px 12px;color:var(--text-3);">Roughly four weeks of onboarding</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
    Two cautions that apply to the category rather than to either vendor. First, <strong>SKU-based pricing compounds
    faster than catalogues feel like they are growing</strong> — adding a few hundred variants can cross a tier, so a
    37% catalogue expansion can arrive as a 100% price rise. Second, merchants have reported meaningful annual
    increases following Sage's acquisition of Inventory Planner, so budget for the trajectory rather than the entry
    price. The trial is the cheap way to resolve the choice: two weeks inside the product tells you more than any
    comparison table, including this one.
  </p>

  <!-- ── choose ─────────────────────────────────────────────────── -->
  <h2 id="choose-h" class="text-2xl font-bold text-white mb-4" style="margin-top:3.5rem;">Which One, Honestly, For Your Store</h2>
  <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
    One question decides almost all of this, and it is not a feature question. It is: <strong>did you use Stocky for
    forecasting, or for tracking?</strong> Answer that honestly — based on what you opened in a normal month, not on
    what the app was capable of — and the rest follows.
  </p>
  <ul class="space-y-3 my-6">
    <li class="text-gray-300"><strong class="text-white">Stock levels, transfers and the occasional PO.</strong> Shopify Admin, natively. You are done, you have spent nothing, and there is no further decision to make.</li>
    <li class="text-gray-300"><strong class="text-white">An order-driven stock ledger you own.</strong> The free Make.com and Google Sheets route. Minutes to deploy, nothing per month, and it does not preclude adding a paid tool later.</li>
    <li class="text-gray-300"><strong class="text-white">Knowing when and how much to reorder.</strong> That is forecasting, and neither Shopify Admin nor the free route does it. Under roughly $200k a month in revenue, the $29–$49 bracket is the sane place to look before the $119 tools.</li>
    <li class="text-gray-300"><strong class="text-white">Selling on Amazon, eBay or Etsy as well as Shopify.</strong> Multi-channel sync is the deciding feature and the free route does not have it. Sumtracker at the affordable end, Cin7 Core at scale.</li>
    <li class="text-gray-300"><strong class="text-white">Manufacturing from raw materials.</strong> None of the general tools track bills of materials, and neither does the free route. Katana or Craftybase is the category you want.</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
    The framing that keeps you out of trouble is symmetrical: <strong>do not buy capability you will not use, and do
    not force a free tool to do something it openly cannot.</strong> Replacing a free tracking app with a $1,440-a-year
    forecasting platform in order to use a fraction of it is the most common expensive mistake here. Not far behind is
    running a spreadsheet ledger and pretending it is a planning system.
  </p>
  <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
    If you are unsure how involved your own move is, the
    <a href="/stocky-migration-risk-scorer/" style="color:var(--g);text-decoration:underline">migration risk scorer</a>
    turns the usage audit into a score in a couple of minutes.
  </p>

  <!-- ── plan / HowTo ───────────────────────────────────────────── -->
  <h2 id="plan-h" class="text-2xl font-bold text-white mb-4" style="margin-top:3.5rem;">The Replacement Sequence</h2>
  <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
    Six steps, in order. Done in sequence this is roughly an hour of real work spread across a verification period.
    Done out of order — picking a tool before auditing usage — is how stores end up paying for forecasting they never
    open.
  </p>
  <ol class="space-y-4 my-6" style="padding-left:0;list-style:none;">
    {howToSteps.map((s, i) => (
      <li id={`plan-step-${i + 1}`} style="border-left:2px solid var(--border);padding-left:16px;">
        <div style="font-family:var(--mono);font-size:10px;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:.12em;color:var(--g);text-transform:uppercase;margin-bottom:6px;">Step {i + 1}</div>
        <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-white mb-2">{s.heading}</h3>
        <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed" style="margin:0;">{s.text}</p>
      </li>
    ))}
  </ol>
  <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
    Step six is the one people skip, and it is the one that catches the problems. Running two records side by side for
    a few weeks is dull and it is also the only way you find out that a location was excluded, an integration was
    silently failing, or a SKU mapping was wrong — while you still have the old numbers to compare against.
  </p>

